  "worked_solution": "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-58 & 136\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
